implementationPlan.note:
DOCUMENTATION ONLY, no behaviour change.

Added a `### moe.reattach_attempt` section to docs/MCP_SERVER.md immediately after `moe.finalize_attempt` (the other runner-called lifecycle tool), in the existing purpose / Parameters / Returns / Notes / Errors format.

The notes carry what a runner cannot guess:
- a daemon restart parks every `running` attempt in `reconciling` BEFORE the purge and holds its task instead of releasing it, spelled out as a deliberate change from the old purge-everything startup, including what "spared" means concretely (record on disk, map entry, currentTaskId, task still WORKING and assigned);
- `reconciling` means the daemon has LOST SIGHT of the execution — not alive, not dead, the absence of both — and the hold consults no idle signal in either direction;
- all four identity elements must match exactly, with `processStartedAt` compared as the exact stored string and never parsed to a date, and a hint-less attempt unmatched;
- the honest boundary, in its own bullet: a match narrows WHICH PROCESS is meant and is NOT evidence the process is alive; nothing may treat it as proof;
- both refusals named — `ATTEMPT_IDENTITY_MISMATCH` for a wrong identity and `ATTEMPT_RECONCILING` for a competing claim — with the scope difference from the finalizing hold (by TASK, not by caller) and the wire note that `context` is not forwarded so callers key on `codeName`;
- reattaching an already-running attempt is a byte-identical successful no-op, so a retry after a lost response is safe;
- it writes only the attempt, never the task or the worker record.
Plus a full `**Errors.**` list (ATTEMPT_NOT_FOUND, ATTEMPT_ID_TASK_MISMATCH, ATTEMPT_IDENTITY_MISMATCH, ATTEMPT_NOT_REATTACHABLE, INVALID_INPUT, MISSING_REQUIRED).

Added one paragraph to the `moe.deregister_worker` daemon-lifecycle prose noting the restart purge now spares a worker owning a non-closed attempt, so worker identity survives a restart, and that every other worker is purged exactly as before.

The reconcile WINDOW and any timeout are deliberately NOT documented — they belong to task-d7fa98f4e43d434a96cad9440b153f1c. Grepped the file: no `reconcile window`/timeout wording was introduced.
